<GuideLayout
  title="Private Label Activewear MOQ Guide: 100 vs 500 vs 1000 Pieces"
  type="MOQ GUIDE"
  description="Compare 100, 500 and 1000 piece MOQ plans for private label activewear by launch risk, unit cost, branding control, inventory pressure and reorder strategy."
>
  <h2>MOQ Should Match Your Launch Stage</h2>
  <p>MOQ is not just a factory rule. It shapes cash flow, inventory risk, unit cost, fabric access and how quickly a brand can test the market. A good MOQ plan protects the first launch while leaving room to reorder winners.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>MOQ Level</th>
        <th>Best Use Case</th>
        <th>Main Advantage</th>
        <th>Main Risk</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>100 pcs</td>
        <td>Market testing, influencer seeding, first private label drop</td>
        <td>Low inventory risk and faster decision making</td>
        <td>Higher unit cost and fewer customization options</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>500 pcs</td>
        <td>First commercial order with clearer demand</td>
        <td>Better unit cost, stronger size/color planning</td>
        <td>Requires better demand forecasting</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>1000 pcs</td>
        <td>Established hero products and repeat orders</td>
        <td>Better pricing and production efficiency</td>
        <td>Higher inventory pressure if the style is unproven</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When 100 Pieces Makes Sense</h2>
  <p>A 100 piece order is useful when the brand is still testing sizing, color demand, creator feedback or retail positioning. It works best for ready-to-ship private label styles or limited customization where fabric and base design already exist.</p>

  <h2>When 500 Pieces Is the Better First Commercial Order</h2>
  <p>For many activewear brands, 500 pieces is the point where planning becomes more commercial. The buyer can split sizes more realistically, negotiate better unit cost and build a stronger launch inventory without jumping too quickly into heavy stock risk.</p>

  <h2>When 1000 Pieces Improves Margin</h2>
  <p>A 1000 piece order is usually best for proven hero products: black leggings, core sports bras, signature yoga sets or styles with repeat demand. At this level, the factory can plan fabric, trims and labor more efficiently, which helps improve unit pricing.</p>

  <h2>MOQ Planning Checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Start with fewer colors and stronger core sizes.</li>
    <li>Do not spread a small MOQ across too many styles.</li>
    <li>Separate testing orders from commercial reorder plans.</li>
    <li>Confirm fabric availability before promoting a launch date.</li>
    <li>Ask whether logo, label and packaging options change at each MOQ level.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fitclo Recommendation</h2>
  <p>If your brand is new, start with 100 to 300 pieces for focused testing. If you already have audience data, 500 pieces can be a stronger launch quantity. Reserve 1000 pieces for proven products where better pricing and stable supply matter more than flexibility.</p>
</GuideLayout>
